Failure to conceive >12 months, Irregular menstrual cycles, Diminished ovarian reserve

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-019
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe lower pelvic pain (dysmenorrhoea flare) and acute psychological crisis following the onset of delayed menses.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and husband
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute, incapacitating pelvic cramping and hyperventilation secondary to a severe panic attack regarding ongoing fertility failure. Urgent assessment was required to provide potent analgesia for the physical pain, rule out an early miscarriage or ectopic pregnancy, and execute acute psychiatric de-escalation to prevent self-harm or emergency department transfer before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The pain is unbearable, but it is the realization that I am bleeding again after being late that is making me unable to breathe.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began two hours ago with the onset of heavy menstrual bleeding after a 5-day delay. The patient reports an agonizing, continuous 3-year history of inability to achieve a clinical pregnancy despite regular, unprotected sexual intercourse. She notes a long history of irregular menstrual cycles suggesting oligo-ovulation. Recently, specialists confirmed advancing maternal age with diminishing ovarian reserve. Tonight, the onset of flow triggered severe dysmenorrhoea (indicative of underlying suspected endometriosis) and an acute, overwhelming psychological crisis. She is hyperventilating, sobbing uncontrollably, and reports a history of recurrent early pregnancy loss, compounding tonight's trauma.
Functional Status: Curled tightly on the bed, physically and emotionally incapacitated.
Social/Safety Concerns: Acute psychological distress; husband is highly supportive but unable to de-escalate the panic.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 26 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Patient is acutely distressed, hyperventilating, crying heavily, and clutching her pelvis.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is soft, with diffuse, severe suprapubic tenderness to deep palpation, but no rebound, rigidity, or guarding. Bowel sounds are normal. Respiratory exam reveals rapid, shallow breathing consistent with acute panic; lungs are clear. Point-of-care urine pregnancy test is definitively negative.
Environmental Observations: Neatly kept home; fertility charting and ovulation kits visible on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Took ibuprofen 400mg one hour ago with no relief for the physical pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Primary/Secondary Dysmenorrhoea complicated by Acute Panic Attack (Background: Primary Infertility) - Status: Acute Crisis
Differentials:

Ruptured Ectopic Pregnancy - Definitively ruled out by the negative point-of-care urine pregnancy test.

Early Spontaneous Miscarriage - Ruled out by the negative highly sensitive urine beta-hCG test; bleeding is an onset of delayed menses, not a failed clinical pregnancy.

Pelvic Inflammatory Disease - Unlikely due to the absence of fever, lack of purulent discharge history, and the clear cyclical (albeit delayed) onset of the current pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to acute psychological crisis)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents in a state of acute physical and psychological crisis. The physical component (severe dysmenorrhoea) and the psychological trigger (onset of menses confirming another failed conception cycle in the context of known diminished ovarian reserve) combined to cause clinical hyperventilation and incapacitating distress. Objective testing definitively ruled out ectopic pregnancy and miscarriage. The absolute necessity to administer parenteral analgesia for the severe cramping, combined with the need for immediate, structured psychological de-escalation to break the panic cycle, strictly required urgent after-hours intervention. Delayed assessment would have resulted in unmanageable pain and a highly traumatic, unnecessary emergency department psychiatric presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular ketorolac 30mg for the acute dysmenorrhoea. Executed guided box-breathing exercises for 15 minutes, successfully slowing the respiratory rate to 16 bpm and resolving the hyperventilation. Prescribed oral mefenamic acid 500mg three times daily.
Positive Behavioral Change: To immediately halt a panic attack, breathe into cupped hands or a paper bag for two minutes; feeling the physical sensation of your heart rate slowing will reinforce your ability to self-regulate acute anxiety. To manage the physical pain spikes, apply a high-heat wrap to your lower abdomen continuously; experiencing the muscular relaxation will encourage reliance on physical therapies alongside medication.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral for urgent psychological support/fertility counseling.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours for a psychological welfare check and to discuss ongoing fertility specialist pathways.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if you develop thoughts of self-harm, if the pain becomes a sudden, sharp, one-sided tearing sensation, or if the bleeding becomes heavy enough to soak through a thick pad in less than 30 minutes.

